    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine what number is a multiple of every integer, we need to understand the definition of a multiple. A number \\( n \\) is a multiple of another number \\( m \\) if there exists an integer \\( k \\) such that \\( n = m \\cdot k \\).\n\nFor a number to be a multiple of every integer, it must be a multiple of 1 (the smallest positive integer). This is because any integer \\( n \\) can be written as \\( m \\cdot 1 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Therefore, \\( n \\) is a multiple of every integer.\n\nTo confirm, let's consider a few examples:\n- The number 1 is a multiple of every integer because \\( 1 = 1 \\cdot k \\) for any integer \\( k \\).\n- The number 2 is a multiple of every even integer and every integer that is a multiple of 2, but not every odd integer.\n- The number 3 is a multiple of every integer that is a multiple of 3, but not every integer that is not a multiple of 3.\n- And so on.\n\nHowever, the only number that is a multiple of every integer is 0, because \\( 0 = m \\cdot 0 \\) for any integer \\( m \\). But we need to check if 0 qualifies as a multiple of every integer in a broader sense.\n\nIf we take any integer \\( n \\), we can write \\( n = 0 \\cdot k \\) for \\( k = 1 \\). This shows that 0 is a multiple of every integer.\n\nTherefore, the number that is a multiple of every integer is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
